<p>During North Dakota's early statehood, the Missouri River was the region's principal commercial transportation route. Even after the railroad arrived in the 1880s, the river remained an important north–south route until development of the national highway system in the mid-1930s.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/rockhaven-harbor/356-1894-usace-map-mandan-area.jpg" alt="1894 Corps of Engineers map showing the Mandan area">
    <figcaption>Corps of Engineers river map, 1894</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Steamboats burned coal and, when coal was unavailable, wood purchased from farmers along the river. One refueling point was owned by {% person "james-bellows" %}James Bellows{% endperson %}, who became president of Mandan's first village board in 1882. Bellows Landing, seven miles northwest of what became downtown Mandan, included an icehouse. Historical records suggest that the area served steamboats as early as 1832, when the <em>Yellowstone</em> reached Fort Union. Regular Missouri River steamboat service began in 1860.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/rockhaven-harbor/334-post-office.jpg" alt="Historic post office associated with Rock Haven harbor">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The harbor was renamed Rock Haven after the U.S. government assumed control in the late 1870s. The Army Corps of Engineers added dry-dock and boat-repair facilities to support supply ships serving the Army's frontier forts. Considered the river's best landing, the site remained permanent and safe during the spring ice breakup that threatened most harbors. Mandan's new federal building and post office, designed in 1912, included space for the harbormaster.</p>

<p>Snagboats were essential to river traffic. Crews cut off tree stumps caught in the channel so the remaining wood could float harmlessly downstream; exceptionally large trees were dynamited and cut apart.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/rockhaven-harbor/444-1912-snagboat-usace-mandan.jpg" alt="Corps of Engineers snagboat Mandan in 1912">
    <figcaption>U.S. Army Corps of Engineers snagboat <em>Mandan</em>, 1912</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The government paddlewheel snagboat <em>Mandan</em> was launched at Yankton, South Dakota, in June 1893. Operating between Williston, North Dakota, and Sioux City, Iowa, it kept the channel clear for commercial traffic. Using ropes, pulleys, chains, and large hooks, its crew steamed toward snags, caught them, and pulled them free.</p>

<p>Other well-known vessels included the freighters <em>Josephine</em>, built at Yankton at the same time as the <em>Mandan</em>, <em>Far West</em>, and <em>John Ordway</em>, along with keelboats, dugouts, buffalo-hide bullboats, and flat-bottomed mackinaws.</p>

