<p>{% person "john-d-anderson" %}John D. Anderson{% endperson %} served as Rock Haven's harbormaster for more than 25 years. Activity declined as steamboat transportation waned during the 1920s.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/rockhaven-harbor/334-1955-rock-haven-sep-3-wr-wood-photoist.jpg" alt="Former Rock Haven site reclaimed in 1955">
    <figcaption>Former Rock Haven site, 1955</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Anderson's daughter {% person "mary-anderson-wunders" %}Mary{% endperson %} married {% person "charles-wunders" %}Charles Wunders{% endperson %}, who spent more than 35 years as an engineer aboard the <em>Mandan</em>. The couple eventually retired near Sioux City, Iowa. Anderson concluded his government career supervising Army Corps of Engineers bank-stabilization work, including placement of riprap at Rock Haven. He and his wife, {% person "alma-bruns-anderson" %}Alma Bruns Anderson{% endperson %}, lived at the harbor. After Anderson died in 1934, the facilities were dismantled and sold.</p>

<p>The Zachmeier family, who lived nearby, purchased the closed site. In 1953, they sold the property's southern portion to Montana-Dakota Utilities, which built the coal-fired Heskett Plant there in 1954.</p>
